04 – Success Metrics and Acceptance Criteria
1. Document Purpose

This document defines the measurable success criteria and acceptance conditions for the B2B Order Management Platform.

The purpose is to determine whether the implemented system meets the defined business, functional, technical, security, and usability requirements.

2. Success Measurement Approach

Project success will be evaluated using the following areas:

Functional completeness
API functionality
Database reliability
Authentication and authorization
Data validation
Order and inventory consistency
Error handling
Testing results
User experience
Documentation quality
Evidence of implementation and validation

The project will be considered successful when the core business workflow operates correctly and the defined acceptance criteria are satisfied.

3. Success Metrics

| Metric ID | Success Metric       | Measurement Method                               | Target                                         |
| --------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| SM-01     | User Registration    | Test valid and invalid registration scenarios    | Valid registration works successfully          |
| SM-02     | User Login           | Test valid and invalid credentials               | Authorized users can log in                    |
| SM-03     | Authentication       | Verify JWT-based protected access                | Protected APIs reject unauthenticated requests |
| SM-04     | Role-Based Access    | Test Customer and Administrator permissions      | Unauthorized role access is blocked            |
| SM-05     | Product Management   | Test create, read, update, and delete operations | Admin product operations work correctly        |
| SM-06     | Inventory Management | Test stock updates and validation                | Stock remains consistent                       |
| SM-07     | Cart Management      | Test add, update, and remove operations          | Cart operations work correctly                 |
| SM-08     | Order Processing     | Test complete order workflow                     | Valid orders are created successfully          |
| SM-09     | Stock Validation     | Attempt orders exceeding available stock         | Invalid quantities are rejected                |
| SM-10     | Order History        | Verify customer-specific orders                  | Customers can view their own orders            |
| SM-11     | Invoice Generation   | Generate invoice for a valid order               | Invoice information is generated correctly     |
| SM-12     | Audit Activity       | Perform tracked administrative actions           | Important activities are recorded              |
| SM-13     | Input Validation     | Test missing and invalid values                  | Invalid input is rejected                      |
| SM-14     | Error Handling       | Test expected failure scenarios                  | Clear error responses are provided             |
| SM-15     | API Integration      | Test frontend-backend communication              | Required API requests work correctly           |
| SM-16     | Database Operations  | Test CRUD and relational operations              | Data is stored and retrieved correctly         |
| SM-17     | Testing Evidence     | Maintain test cases and results                  | Core functionality has documented evidence     |
| SM-18     | Documentation        | Review project documentation                     | Required documents are complete and organized  |


4. Functional Acceptance Criteria
4.1 User Registration

The registration functionality will be accepted when:

A new customer can register successfully.
Required fields are validated.
Invalid email formats are rejected.
Password confirmation is validated.
Duplicate email registration is prevented.
Appropriate success and error messages are displayed.


4.2 User Login

The login functionality will be accepted when:

Registered users can log in using valid credentials.
Invalid credentials are rejected.
A valid JWT token is generated after successful authentication.
The user's role is identified correctly.
Users are redirected to the appropriate area of the application.


4.3 Role-Based Access Control

The authorization functionality will be accepted when:

Customer users can access customer features.
Administrator users can access administrative features.
Customers cannot access restricted administrator APIs.
Unauthorized requests are rejected.
Role information is correctly associated with authenticated users.


4.4 Product Management

Product management will be accepted when an administrator can:

Create a product.
View products.
Update product information.
Delete a product when appropriate.
Enter valid product information.
Receive appropriate validation and error messages.


4.5 Inventory Management

Inventory management will be accepted when:

Administrators can view product stock.
Administrators can update stock quantities.
Invalid stock values are rejected.
Stock information is correctly stored in the database.
Product availability reflects the current stock level.


4.6 Cart Management

Cart functionality will be accepted when:

Customers can add products to the cart.
Customers can update product quantities.
Customers can remove products.
The system calculates the cart total correctly.
The system prevents invalid quantities.
The system checks product availability.


4.7 Order Processing

Order processing will be accepted when:

A customer can place an order containing valid products.
The system validates product availability before creating the order.
The order is stored correctly in the database.
The order is associated with the correct customer.
Product stock is updated after a successful order.
An appropriate order status is assigned.
Failed orders do not result in incorrect stock changes.


4.8 Order History

Order history will be accepted when:

Customers can view their own orders.
Orders contain correct order information.
Customers cannot access another customer's order information.
Order status is displayed correctly.
Empty order history is handled appropriately.


4.9 Invoice Generation

Invoice functionality will be accepted when:

An invoice can be generated for a valid order.
Customer information is displayed correctly.
Order information is displayed correctly.
Product and quantity information is included.
Total amount is calculated correctly.
Invoice generation errors are handled appropriately.


4.10 Audit Activity

Audit functionality will be accepted when:

Important administrative actions are recorded.
Audit records contain relevant information.
Activity date and time are recorded.
The responsible user can be identified.
Administrators can review available audit records.

5. Technical Acceptance Criteria

The project will meet technical acceptance requirements when:

The frontend communicates with the backend through REST APIs.
Backend APIs follow a consistent structure.
PostgreSQL is used for persistent data storage.
Database tables and relationships are correctly implemented.
Database migrations are maintained where applicable.
JWT authentication is implemented for protected resources.
Role-based authorization is implemented.
Server-side validation is implemented.
Appropriate HTTP status codes are returned.
Errors are handled without exposing sensitive implementation details.
Environment-specific configuration is separated from source code.
Sensitive credentials are not committed to the GitHub repository.
6. Security Acceptance Criteria

The security requirements will be accepted when:

Passwords are not stored as plain text.
Authentication is required for protected resources.
JWT tokens are validated before accessing protected APIs.
Role-based restrictions are enforced on the backend.
Unauthorized users receive appropriate responses.
Input validation is applied to important API requests.
Database credentials and secret keys are stored securely.
Sensitive configuration files are excluded from version control where required.
7. Testing Acceptance Criteria

Testing will be considered satisfactory when:

Core functional workflows have been tested.
Both successful and unsuccessful scenarios are tested.
Validation scenarios are tested.
Authentication scenarios are tested.
Authorization scenarios are tested.
Product and inventory operations are tested.
Cart and order operations are tested.
API responses are verified.
Database operations are verified.
Important defects identified during testing are fixed or documented.
8. Evidence Requirements

The project will maintain evidence to demonstrate implementation and validation.

Evidence may include:

Application screenshots
API testing screenshots
Database screenshots
Test case results
Git commit history
GitHub repository evidence
Validation results
Error-handling demonstrations
Order workflow demonstration
Authentication and authorization testing
Mentor feedback
Final project demonstration

Evidence files should be stored in the evidence directory using clear and descriptive filenames.

9. Business Workflow Acceptance

The complete business workflow will be considered successful when the following sequence can be demonstrated:

Customer Registration → Login → Browse Products → Add to Cart → Checkout → Place Order → Inventory Update → View Order → Generate Invoice

The administrator workflow should demonstrate:

Admin Login → Manage Products → Manage Inventory → Manage Orders → Review Audit Activities

10. Definition of Done

A feature will be considered complete when:

The feature is implemented.
Required validation is implemented.
The feature is connected to the appropriate API or database functionality.
Successful scenarios have been tested.
Failure scenarios have been tested.
Identified critical issues have been resolved or documented.
Evidence has been captured where applicable.
The code has been committed to GitHub.
Relevant documentation has been updated.
11. Project Completion Criteria

The project will be considered ready for final submission when:

Core frontend functionality is implemented.
Backend APIs are implemented.
PostgreSQL database integration is completed.
Authentication and authorization are functional.
Core product, cart, inventory, and order workflows are functional.
Validation and error handling are implemented.
Testing evidence is available.
Documentation is organized.
GitHub repository is organized and reproducible.
README provides setup and usage instructions.
Final demonstration can explain the problem, solution, implementation, testing, limitations, and future improvements.


12. Limitations

The project may have limitations due to the internship timeline and development environment.

Potential limitations may include:

No real payment gateway integration.
No real business production deployment.
Use of simulated or development data.
Limited number of test users.
Limited load and performance testing.
Some advanced features may remain future enhancements.

These limitations will be clearly documented rather than treated as completed functionality.

13. Future Enhancements

Possible future enhancements include:

Online payment integration
Email and SMS notifications
Advanced reporting dashboard
Product search and filtering
Advanced analytics
Automated testing pipeline
Cloud deployment
Docker-based deployment
Improved monitoring and logging
Mobile application integration


